    "topic_title": "Estimating Derivatives of a Function at a Point",
    "stem": "A balloon’s height h(t), in meters, is differentiable, where t is measured in seconds. Selected values are given: t = 0, h(t) = 2; t = 4, h(t) = 14; t = 6, h(t) = 22; t = 10, h(t) = 52. Using the closest listed times on opposite sides of t = 5, which estimate of h′(5) is obtained?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "h′(5) ≈ 5 m/s",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Distant-data trap: (52−2)/(10−0) = 5 uses the table’s endpoints rather than the closest times surrounding 5. That wider interval does not give the requested local estimate. Fix: Select the nearest listed time below 5 and the nearest above 5."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "h′(5) ≈ 8 m/s",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Missing-denominator trap: 22−14 = 8 is the height change in meters, not a rate in meters per second. The elapsed time is 6−4 = 2 seconds. Fix: Divide the height change by the corresponding elapsed time."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "h′(5) ≈ 4 m/s",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The closest listed times surrounding 5 are 4 and 6, giving the local slope (22−14)/(6−4) = 4 m/s. This secant slope estimates the instantaneous rate at t = 5."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "h′(5) ≈ 18 m/s",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Average-height trap: (14+22)/2 = 18 averages heights, so its units are meters rather than meters per second. A derivative estimate requires a slope, not an average function value. Fix: Compute the change in height divided by the change in time."
     }
    ]
   },
