   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-bc",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.3",
    "topic_title": "Estimating Derivatives of a Function at a Point",
    "stem": "A cup of tea is cooling. The table gives its temperature T(t), in degrees Celsius, at selected times t, in minutes. t: 0, 2, 5, 6, 9. T(t): 70, 64, 58, 55, 49. Using the data at the nearest table times on either side of t = 5, which is the best estimate of T′(5)?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "−7/3 °C per minute",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This uses the distant endpoints t = 0 and t = 9, giving (49 − 70)/9, the average rate over the whole table. Values far from t = 5 describe the overall trend, not the local rate at t = 5. Fix: Pick the table values closest to the target time on each side before computing the slope."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "−9 °C per minute",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This finds the temperature change 55 − 64 but never divides by the elapsed time of 4 minutes. A change in temperature is measured in °C, not °C per minute, so it is not a rate. Fix: Divide the output change by the input change and check that the units read °C per minute."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "−2.25 °C per minute",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The nearest times bracketing t = 5 are t = 2 and t = 6, so T′(5) ≈ (55 − 64)/(6 − 2) = −9/4 = −2.25. This difference quotient is a local slope estimate in °C per minute, not an exact derivative value."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "2.25 °C per minute",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is a sign error from subtracting temperatures and times in opposite orders, such as (64 − 55)/(6 − 2). The temperature is falling between t = 2 and t = 6, so the rate must be negative. Fix: Subtract outputs and inputs in the same order, then check whether the sign matches increasing or decreasing data."
     }
    ]
   },
